  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nLast night's convection has shunted the frontal boundary south of \nthe area, with current surface obs placing it along a line from \nsoutheast KS into the TX Panhandle. Low stratus is locked in place \nnorth of the boundary, keeping temperatures nice and cool in the 50s \nto low 60s. Weak isentropic ascent is keeping some isolated showers \nand pockets of drizzle around, but rain coverage should be limited \nuntil the upper low begins to approach overnight. The heaviest rain \nstill looks tied to a weak surface wave that will lift northeast \nacross eastern KS. As mentioned yesterday, the extensive convection \nand heavy rain to our south over Oklahoma is often a setup that \nleads to a southward trend in QPF guidance. This appears to be such \na case. Most guidance now keeps the strongest moisture advection and \nisentropic ascent - and thus the best chance for an additional 1-3 \ninches of rain - a bit to our southeast. Will keep Anderson County \nin the Flood Watch, especially since southern parts of the county \nsaw a radar-estimated 2-3\" of rain last night, but have trimmed out \nthe rest of the watch. Elsewhere across the forecast area lesser \namounts are expected, though the track of the upper low should still \nlead to widespread 0.25-0.5\" amounts with pockets of slightly above \n1\". Temperatures tomorrow should rise into the 70s across east-\ncentral KS (south of wherever the surface low eventually tracks) but \nstay in the 60s to upper 50s elsewhere.\n\nThe cold front trailing behind the surface low will pass through by \nThursday evening, allowing temperatures and dewpoints to quickly \ndrop overnight. Strong high pressure will push southeast across the \nPlains as a strong northern stream shortwave drops out of Canada and \nphases with the southern stream upper low. Strong CAA should keep \nFriday highs in the 50s. By Saturday morning, the high pressure will \nbe nearly overhead. With strong radiational cooling amidst clear \nskies and light winds, temperatures should fall into the low/mid 30s \narea wide. The best chance at sub-freezing temperatures looks to be \nin favored low-lying areas north of Interstate 70. Will likely need \nFrost/Freeze headlines at some point, though will wait another day \nor so to figure out exact areas.\n\nAfter high pressure passes off to our east later Saturday, we'll see \na gradual trend back to warmer temperatures as ridging builds back \nover the central/eastern CONUS. Expecting highs back in the 70s by \nMonday and Tuesday. Still can't rule out a brief shower sometime \nMonday with a weak shortwave passing through, but overall rain \nchances look low through Tuesday.",
